  "Number": 8,
  "Question": "Two years ago Pete was three times as old as his cousin Claire. Two years before that, Pete was four times as old as Claire. In how many years will the ratio of their ages be \\(2:1?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(2\\)",
    "B": "\\(4\\)",
    "C": "\\(5\\)",
    "D": "\\(6\\)",
    "E": "\\(8\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Start at the earlier time so that both later ages can be expressed by adding the same number of years. Let \\(c>0\\) be Claire's age four years ago. Pete was then \\(4c\\).\n\nTwo years later, their ages were \\(c+2\\) and \\(4c+2\\), and Pete was three times Claire's age. Thus\n\\[\n4c+2=3(c+2)=3c+6,\n\\]\nso \\(c=4\\). Their current ages are therefore \\(8\\) and \\(20\\), respectively.\n\nLet \\(t\\geq 0\\) be the number of years from now until Pete is twice Claire's age. Then\n\\[\n20+t=2(8+t)=16+2t,\n\\]\nwhich gives \\(t=4\\). At that time they will be \\(24\\) and \\(12\\), so the answer is \\(\\boxed{4}\\) years, choice \\(\\text{B}\\).",
